8. Why does this BigDecimal code throw an exception? Fix it and explain.
BigDecimal a = new BigDecimal("10");
BigDecimal b = new BigDecimal("3");
BigDecimal result = a.divide(b);
System.out.println(result);

items.a:
a.divide(b) throws ArithmeticException: 'Non-terminating decimal expansion; no exact representable decimal result.' 10 ÷ 3 = 3.333... (infinitely repeating) — BigDecimal cannot represent this exactly without a specified scale. Fixes:
// Fix 1: specify scale and rounding mode
BigDecimal result = a.divide(b, 6, RoundingMode.HALF_UP);
System.out.println(result); // 3.333333

// Fix 2: use MathContext for significant figures
BigDecimal result2 = a.divide(b, new MathContext(10, RoundingMode.HALF_UP));
System.out.println(result2); // 3.333333333

Rule: always specify RoundingMode in BigDecimal.divide() — plain divide() only works for terminating decimals (e.g., 1/4 = 0.25).
